{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>אֶת כֻּתָּנְתּוֹ אֶת כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו.</b> Rashi explained: ״כֻּתָּנְתּוֹ״ — this is his ordinary tunic; and why did they strip off his tunic? And perhaps Joseph, in going to his brothers, was afraid lest his brothers tear the tunic of fine stripes from him by force; therefore he did a youthful thing and went and sewed and joined the tunic of fine stripes to his ordinary tunic, for he said, presumably they would not present him naked, and if so he did not suspect them of this. Therefore they stripped off even his ordinary tunic along with the tunic of fine stripes, and the word ״אֶת״ would mean ״עִם״. And some say that Joseph exchanged his tunics and dressed the tunic of fine stripes directly upon his body, and his ordinary tunic he put on above, in order to conceal the tunic of fine stripes from his brothers; therefore it says ״אֶת כֻּתָּנְתּוֹ״, for at first they stripped off the ordinary tunic that was on top, and afterward אֶת כְּתֹנֶת הַפַּסִּים אֲשֶׁר עָלָיו, which was directly upon his body, and not on top of something else."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:20.052353271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}